1
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:04,040
Everything that matters
happened at some point in time:

2
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:08,932
a war, a story you're writing,
the hour your server went down.

3
00:00:10,392 --> 00:00:13,782
OpenChronology puts them
on one line you can zoom,

4
00:00:13,832 --> 00:00:17,002
from billions of years down
to a fraction of a second.

5
00:00:17,052 --> 00:00:22,532
Every time is stored exactly, as a fraction,
so nothing blurs or rounds however

6
00:00:22,582 --> 00:00:23,853
far you go.

7
00:00:25,303 --> 00:00:30,553
Students follow a country's history.
Writers keep their story's chronology

8
00:00:30,603 --> 00:00:34,663
straight. Teachers show four and
a half billion years of Earth,

9
00:00:34,713 --> 00:00:38,404
and how thin the slice holding
all of human history is.

10
00:00:39,974 --> 00:00:45,214
It's free software. Use it on
the website, in the desktop app,

11
00:00:45,264 --> 00:00:48,744
or as a single file that
never touches the network.

12
00:00:48,794 --> 00:00:53,605
Start with how to read a timeline,
then pick the video for what you do.
